WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.220
So today we're going to talk
about something really profound.

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:05.680
We're not looking at organ
donation as some cold,

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:10.240
clinical procedure, but as what it
really is: a deeply human story.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:14.200
This is the story of a gift
and the incredible journey it takes.

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:16.780
Let me just start by
asking you a question.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:19.420
What do you think is the single greatest

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:21.880
gift one person can
possibly give to another?

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:23.500
It's a pretty big question, right?

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:27.280
Well, the answer is even bigger,
and it really comes in two parts.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:31.000
Okay, so part one of the answer,
it's this number:

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:35.560
a single organ donor can give seven
or more life-saving organs.

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:39.580
We're talking about hearts, lungs,
kidneys, livers, giving people

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:42.760
who are right on the brink a
second chance at life itself.

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:44.740
And that's not even the whole story.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:48.250
The second part of that answer is over 65.

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:51.280
That's how many lives can
be completely transformed

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:53.860
through tissue donation from
that very same person.

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:56.260
I mean, we're talking
about giving sight back,

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:58.960
restoring mobility, saving burn victims.

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:02.080
The scale of what one decision
can do is just staggering.

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:05.080
But, you know,
to really get why this gift is

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:07.900
so incredible, we first
have to understand the need.

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:11.740
Who are the people waiting,
hoping for that life-altering call?

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:15.760
Look, behind these clinical terms,
these are real people.

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:20.200
Someone with end-stage organ failure
isn't just a diagnosis on a chart.

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:22.840
They're a person whose
body just can't keep going,

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:25.300
whether it's their kidneys or their heart,

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:27.700
or someone with such advanced
lung disease they have

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:30.100
less than a fifty-fifty
chance of surviving two years.

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:32.980
For them, a transplant
isn't just an option.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:34.420
It is their only hope.

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:37.840
And it's the same for severe burn
victims who need skin to live,

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:39.880
or for those living in darkness just

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:41.500
waiting for corneas to
be able to see again.

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:46.180
Okay, so how does this whole journey
from a donor to a recipient even begin?

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:49.780
Well, it all starts at a
very specific and honestly

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:52.720
a very misunderstood medical
and legal moment.

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:56.740
So let's get really clear on exactly
when donation can become a possibility.

00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:00.820
The key concept we absolutely
have to understand is

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:03.400
brain death, and it's
so important to get this right.

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:04.720
This is not a coma.

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:05.860
It's not a vegetative state.

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:10.360
It is the complete, total, and
irreversible end of all brain function.

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:14.680
A person who is brain-dead is legally
and medically dead. Period.

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:18.760
And this image right here shows you
exactly why brain death is so final.

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:23.140
That highlighted part, the brainstem,
that's the body's command center.

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:26.200
It controls everything,
and I mean everything that's essential

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:29.560
for life, including our most
basic function: breathing.

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:33.640
When it stops working for good, the
body simply cannot function on its own,

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:36.160
even if machines are temporarily
keeping the oxygen flowing.

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:40.950
And if there's any lingering doubt,
the medical certainty here is absolute.

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:42.940
This quote is incredibly important.

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:47.380
It confirms that no one,
not a single person who has been

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:51.510
correctly diagnosed as brain-dead,
has ever recovered. Ever.

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:53.980
The diagnosis is final.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:59.080
So based on that, there are really two
main paths for deceased organ donation.

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:03.700
The first and the most common one
is donation after brain death,

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:05.140
which is what we've
just been talking about.

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:09.040
The second is something called
donation after circulatory death.

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:13.000
This can happen in a palliative
care situation when it's known

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:16.000
that death is unavoidable after
life support is withdrawn.

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:19.240
Now you might be thinking this
all sounds very complicated,

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:22.420
but you need to know this process
doesn't just happen in a vacuum.

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:25.840
It's governed by an incredibly
strict legal and ethical framework.

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:28.660
It's all designed to protect
everyone involved and to make

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:31.360
sure the entire process is
handled with total integrity.

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:35.800
In South Africa, that framework is
Chapter Eight of the National Health Act.

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:38.200
This isn't some informal guideline.

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:42.640
It's a matter of national law with
crystal-clear, non-negotiable rules.

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:46.900
And this right here shows you just how
many checks and balances there are.

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:50.500
First, two totally separate
doctors who have absolutely

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:53.200
nothing to do with the transplant
team have to agree on the

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:55.840
diagnosis of brain death,
and one of them has to be pretty

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:58.300
experienced, with at least
five years under their belt.

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:01.420
Then, and only then,
can a transplant coordinator

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:02.920
even approach the family for consent.

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:05.320
And if the death was,
say, from an accident,

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:07.480
a state pathologist has
to sign off on it too.

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:10.060
It's a system with multiple
layers of oversight.

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:13.720
This is something I really want to
emphasize because it's so important.

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:16.000
The teams are kept completely separate.

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:19.780
The doctors who are focused on saving a
patient's life are not the same doctors

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:23.320
who certify death, and they're definitely
not the people involved in a transplant.

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:25.660
This separation is a legal requirement.

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:29.440
It's there to eliminate any possible
conflict of interest and to guarantee

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:33.340
that the patient's care is always,
always the number one priority.

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:37.540
And let's talk about money, because this
is a huge worry for a lot of people.

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:41.620
It's really simple: by law,
there is absolutely no financial

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:44.380
cost to the donor's family
for the act of donation.

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:45.100
Not a cent.

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:48.700
Once death has been declared
and the family has given consent,

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:50.980
all the costs from
that point on are covered.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:53.800
It is a gift in every
single sense of the word.

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:56.860
Okay, so we've talked about
the medicine and the law,

00:04:56.880 --> 00:04:59.920
but now we get to the most human
part of this whole journey.

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:01.900
Because when it's all said
and done, everything

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:04.540
comes down to a single
compassionate conversation.

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:08.200
And this conversation is handled
with such incredible care.

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:11.440
A trained transplant coordinator
gets involved early on,

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:14.080
but the topic of donation
is never ever brought

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:16.840
up until the family has
had time to really absorb

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:18.940
and accept the diagnosis
of death from their doctor.

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:23.140
There's no false hope, no misleading
information, and absolutely no rush.

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:26.620
The family's grief and their
understanding always come first.

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:30.640
And this brings us to what
might be the single most

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:33.400
important piece of information
in this entire explainer.

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:34.840
So please listen closely.

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:39.640
In South Africa, even if you have
registered to be an organ donor,

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:44.020
the final legal authority, the final say,
rests with your next of kin.

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:47.860
Your family must give their
consent for donation to happen.

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:53.380
So after that incredibly difficult
decision is made, what happens next?

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:55.780
What is the legacy of this amazing gift?

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:59.620
Well, the ripple effect is almost
hard to wrap your head around.

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:03.280
And remember, it goes beyond
the life-saving organs.

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:05.800
Tissue donation can be just as miraculous.

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:08.500
Corneas from a donor can
let someone see their

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:11.620
kids' faces again;
donated bone can help a person

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:14.380
walk without pain;
and skin grafts are absolutely

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:16.660
life-saving for people
with terrible burns.

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:21.520
It's about restoring life and the quality
of that life in the most fundamental ways.

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:23.740
So let's bring it all
back to where we started.

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:26.620
This chart just powerfully
sums up the whole story.

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:30.760
One person's decision, supported by their
family in the worst moment of their lives,

00:06:30.780 --> 00:06:33.820
can lead to over seven
lives being saved and more

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:36.940
than 65 lives being healed,
restored, and transformed.

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:40.180
That is a legacy of life,
sight, and movement.

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:43.600
Which really leaves us with
one final personal thought.

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:46.540
Knowing that your family has the
final say, the most important

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:49.660
step you can take isn't just signing
a card or registering online.

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:50.980
It's talking.

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:53.740
You've spent a lifetime sharing
your life with the people you love.

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:56.407
Have you taken a moment
to share your wishes?
